  "rationale": "crown:obtain is an OR of h4_0:escalate, h4_2:pivot and h4_1:reach, so all three must be cut. Blocking those three steps directly does this, so 3 is enough. To show 2 is not enough: h2_0:traverse comes straight from the entry foothold edge0:reach and is a parent of h4_0:escalate, so any defense must block h4_0:escalate or h2_0:traverse. (1) If h4_0:escalate is blocked, h4_2:pivot must still be stopped. Doing that without blocking h4_2:pivot itself needs at least two blocks, because h3_2:escalate needs one block and h2_2:obtain needs another. Blocking h4_2:pivot instead leaves h4_1:reach open, since h2_2:obtain is reached via h1_2:escalate and h2_0:traverse is reached from edge0:reach. (2) If h2_0:traverse is blocked, h4_0:escalate is still reachable through h3_2:escalate (via h1_2:escalate or h1_1:pivot) and through h2_1:escalate. No single further block cuts both of these routes. So 2 blocks always fail, and the minimum is 3 (for example, block h4_0:escalate, h4_2:pivot and h4_1:reach)."
